/**
@page tps_overview TPS: differential algebra for polynomial maps

This C++20 library represents dense multivariate polynomials and finite Taylor
jets for accelerator maps. All public types live in namespace tps; include
`<tps/tps.hpp>`. The CMake target is `TPS::tps`; OPALX links it publicly.

- @subpage tps_mathematics defines coefficients, algebra and map composition.
- @subpage tps_numerics defines precision, domains and implementation limits.
- @subpage code_review records readability changes and remaining risks.
- @subpage error_contracts describes failure reporting and borrowed-storage lifetimes.
- @subpage workflow_audit identifies missing OPALX map-workflow capabilities.
- @subpage map_context_api specifies the context-bearing map API.
- @subpage kokkos_execution explains runtime ownership, storage and numerical backends.
- @subpage prepared_map_api describes immutable plans and allocation-free device evaluation.
- @ref tps::Tps is the scalar polynomial API.
- @ref tps::VpsMap is the vector-valued map API.

@par OPALX integration
The default standard is C++20, matching OPALX. TPS reuses the Kokkos backend
selected through IPPL and uses Kokkos Kernels for host linear algebra. Include
`<tps/tps.hpp>` and link `OPALX::opalx` or `TPS::tps` inside the OPALX build.
Host map construction, composition and analysis must be serialized within each
MPI rank. The current compatibility layer still scopes/restores a global order;
fixed-order enforcement is a separate follow-up. Prepared maps snapshot data for
parallel tracking, with one disjoint workspace per concurrent evaluation.
No automatic OPALX coordinate/unit conversion or particle-loss handling is implied.
The numerical suite is under `unit_tests/tps`; select it with `ctest -L tps`.
Build `tps_docs` for this focused reference, or `opalx_docs` for the full API.

@par Executable example
The example below uses abstract canonical coordinates, not OPALX lab momentum.
Its drift and kick are composed in that order and checked against analytic values.
@include maps.cpp
*/

/**
@page tps_mathematics Mathematical conventions

@section coefficients Coefficients and multi-indices
For @f$n@f$ variables and multi-index @f$\alpha\in\mathbb N_0^n@f$, define
@f$|\alpha|=\sum_j\alpha_j@f$, @f$\alpha!=\prod_j\alpha_j!@f$ and
@f$x^\alpha=\prod_j x_j^{\alpha_j}@f$. An order-@f$p@f$ representation stores
@f[
f(x)=\sum_{|\alpha|\le p} c_\alpha x^\alpha,
\qquad c_\alpha=\frac{\partial^\alpha f(0)}{\alpha!}.
@f]
Coefficients are ordinary polynomial coefficients, NOT raw derivatives.
For instance @f$\exp(x)=1+x+x^2/2+x^3/6+\cdots@f$.
The number of stored coefficients is @f$M(n,p)=\binom{n+p}{p}@f$.
The constant is index zero and the linear coordinates occupy indices 1 through n.
For two variables the graded order begins @f$1,x,y,x^2,xy,y^2@f$.
Prefer TpsMonomial for higher-degree terms rather than assuming an index formula.

@section algebra Polynomial operations
Addition is coefficient-wise. With total-degree projection @f$P_p@f$,
@f[
 (fg)_\alpha=\sum_{\beta+\gamma=\alpha}f_\beta g_\gamma,
 \qquad f\mathbin{\star_p}g=P_p(fg).
@f]
This is total-degree truncation, not a separate per-variable bound.
Exact finite polynomials may grow in degree under multiplication. Finite jets
identify polynomials modulo monomials of degree greater than the chosen order.
Storage and precision are distinct; see @ref tps_numerics.

Differentiation and integration operate on the polynomial representative:
@f[
 (\partial_j f)_\alpha=(\alpha_j+1)c_{\alpha+e_j},\qquad
 \int f\,dx_j=\sum_\alpha\frac{c_\alpha}{\alpha_j+1}x^{\alpha+e_j}.
@f]
Integration sets the integration constant to zero. Derivatives of discarded terms
are unavailable; differentiating an order-p approximation generally reduces its
known accuracy by one order. Precision metadata is not a rigorous error bound.

@section functions Analytic functions and inverse
Write @f$f=a+u@f$, with @f$u(0)=0@f$. For an analytic scalar function @f$h@f$,
@f[
 h(f)=P_p\left(\sum_{k=0}^{p}\frac{h^{(k)}(a)}{k!}u^k\right).
@f]
`Tps::taylor` accepts a `std::span<const T>` of factorial-normalized coefficients.
The Taylor implementation uses a Horner recurrence, truncating intermediate
products. For @f$a\ne0@f$ the inverse is
@f[
 f^{-1}=P_p\left(\frac1a\sum_{k=0}^{p}(-u/a)^k\right).
@f]
An exact finite polynomial usually has no finite polynomial inverse: the result
is a jet at the resolved finite order. Real square root and logarithm currently
require strictly positive constant term. Division/inversion requires nonzero
constant term. No numerical near-zero threshold is introduced.

@section maps Vector maps and substitution direction
A map @f$F:\mathbb R^n\to\mathbb R^m@f$ has one scalar TPS per output.
`F.substitute(G,p)` computes @f$P_p(F\circ G)@f$: G acts first.
For a scalar polynomial, matrix substitution computes @f$f(Mx)@f$.
Matrix multiplication on map outputs represents @f$MF(x)@f$.
For a stored map, its Jacobian is @f$J_{ij}=\partial F_i/\partial x_j@f$;
`linearTerms(point)` evaluates that Jacobian at the point.

If the components are truncated approximations, composition about the same
origin preserves their known order only when the inner map has zero constant
term. A nonzero inner constant can move unknown high-degree terms into low
degrees. The library evaluates the stored polynomial; it cannot reconstruct
those discarded terms or validate convergence away from the expansion point.

For abstract canonical coordinates @f$(q,p)@f$, the example uses
@f$D(q,p)=(q+Lp,p)@f$ and @f$K(q,p)=(q,p-kq^2)@f$.
Their exact polynomial composition is
@f$K\circ D=(q+Lp,p-k(q+Lp)^2)@f$.
Symplecticity requires canonical coordinates and appropriate underlying maps;
TPS arithmetic alone does not establish it for an OPALX tracking model.
*/

/**
@page tps_numerics Precision, domains and implementation

@section orders Order resolution
`getMaxOrder()` is allocated polynomial degree. `getTruncOrder()` is precision
metadata constrained by the global limit. `EXACT` is a sentinel, never an
allocation size. The global default stays EXACT for finite polynomial algebra.
Taylor functions use the finite representation/global/explicit order if one is
set; otherwise they use the input's stored degree. Scalar constants need order
zero. Therefore `exp(makeVariable(...))` defaults to first order; choose a finite
global order or allocate the input at the desired order for higher expansions.
Negative global/explicit inverse orders are errors; zero is supported.

@section coefficients_api Coefficient access and storage
Brackets do not grow storage. Invalid integer or monomial accesses throw
CLRangeError; mismatched variable counts throw SizeError. Explicit setters can
grow polynomial storage. Coefficients are aligned, constructed and destroyed as
T objects in Kokkos-owned HostSpace storage. Copy-on-write avoids copying on a read-only
TPS copy; modifying a copy detaches its representation. Mutable references must
not be retained across operations that replace storage or subsequent sharing.
Generic arrays own Kokkos HostSpace allocations and commit assignment only after a
successful copy. Exceptions derive from std::runtime_error.

@section limits Limits and guarantees
The fixed cache accepts 1..99 variables; zero denotes scalar coefficients.
Dimension and coefficient counts must fit the implementation's integer indexing.
Large representable requests may still exhaust memory. Dense storage and cached
monomial products can be costly; no sparse or device implementation is provided.
Global truncation and mutable caches are NOT thread-safe. Do not concurrently
change limits or build/extend shared tables without external synchronization.
MPI ranks are independent processes; no distributed algorithm is implemented.

The current numerical tests cover double, selected complex arithmetic and nested
TPS arithmetic; not every function supports every coefficient type. In particular
real-domain comparisons in square root/logarithm exclude arbitrary complex
arguments. Coefficients need the operations used by the instantiated algorithm.
No interval bounds, conditioning estimates or convergence guarantees are supplied.
Array and TPS indexed accesses are checked. Raw pointers and iterator element
access retain normal valid-range and lifetime preconditions.
Cache rebuilds and some compound algebra operations do not provide a strong
exception guarantee after arbitrary coefficient-operation/allocation failure.

@section validation Validation and reproducibility
The test suite checks coefficient identities, truncation, map substitution,
boundaries, nontrivial coefficient lifetimes/alignment, copy failures, C++20
iterator concepts and selected ranges algorithms. Low-order numerical checks
use fixed small inputs and an absolute tolerance of 2e-13; that is a test
criterion, not a general accuracy guarantee. The original sources are preserved
under baseline/ in the external opal-tps test bed; it is not imported into OPALX.
*/

/**
@page map_context_api Context-bearing maps and checked composition

@section chart_contract Charts and local coordinates
MapContext owns validated input/output PhaseSpaceChart values, a finite
nonnegative order and optional segment traversal IDs. Each chart has named
coordinates, unit labels, positive scales, finite reference coordinates,
a right-handed orthonormal frame, optional time/momentum references, and a
coordinate convention. Canonical charts explicitly pair all coordinate indices.
Frame axes are stored row-major; frame origins are in metres and time is seconds.
Frames are validated to absolute tolerance 1e-12; this metadata validation
threshold does not change any numerical tracking tolerance.

Map coefficients act on local coordinates, not directly on physical positions:
@f[
 x_i = (X_i-X_{\mathrm{ref},i})/s_i,\qquad
 Y_i=Y_{\mathrm{ref},i}+s'_i F_i(x).
@f]
Frame projection, unit conversion and OPALX-specific canonical transformations
must be performed by adapters. Metadata records their meaning; the library does
not silently apply them. Declaring canonical pairs does not establish symplecticity.

Map snapshots coefficient storage at construction. Components must match input
and output dimensions, fit the context order and possess at least that precision.
All-constant maps retain their declared input dimension through context. Accessors
expose const coefficients/context. Evaluation and Jacobians take spans of local
input values and return local outputs/derivatives. The physical-coordinate
Jacobian additionally requires scale and frame transformations by the caller.

@section composition_contract Composition contract
`compose(outer,inner,p)` computes @f$P_p(\mathrm{outer}\circ\mathrm{inner})@f$;
inner acts first. Input/output chart equality includes names, dimensions,
coordinate units/order/scales, reference values, frame, optional reference time
and momentum, convention and canonical pairing. Equality is exact, not a floating
comparison tolerance. Supply a distinct explicit transformation map when changing
charts. Requested order cannot exceed either context order. Without an explicit
order, composition uses the smaller available order.

Segment provenance is stored in tracking order. IDs identify unique traversal
occurrences, not just element names. Repeated nonempty IDs are rejected to avoid
double-counting shared overlap intervals. Repeated turns require distinct
traversal IDs. Maps with no IDs have no overlap-detection guarantee.

The default CompositionPolicy::preserveOrigin requires all inner constant terms
to be exactly zero. This protects the order interpretation of truncated jets
about a common reference. CompositionPolicy::storedPolynomial explicitly allows
nonzero constants and computes the stored-polynomial result without claiming
accuracy inherited from unknown discarded terms. No small-constant tolerance is
silently introduced. See @ref maps for the translation caveat.

`composeSequence(maps,p)` takes maps in tracking order, first entry first.
Empty sequences are rejected; use `identityMap(chart,p)` for an explicit identity.
Identity requires p>=1; projections/compositions support order zero.
`Map::truncated(p)` projects a map to a lower/equal order without changing charts
or provenance. Bracket coefficient access and explicit component setters remain
in the lower-level algebra API; a context-bearing Map itself is immutable.

@section context_execution Execution and limitations
The initial context layer scopes/restores the legacy scalar global order on all
success/failure paths. It prebuilds the input-variable monomial cache before
substitution to avoid table invalidation during nonlinear products. This is not
a thread-safety solution: shared caches and global settings still need external
synchronization. A context does not make a direct TPS field integrator, validity
region, map inverse, normal-form decomposition or split space-charge tracker.
Those remain separate stages before full OPALX integration.

@section context_example Example
The example is compiled and executed as a regression. No scalar global-order
initialization is required for these map operations.
@include context_maps.cpp
*/

/**
@page kokkos_execution Kokkos storage and linear algebra

@section execution_dependencies Dependency and runtime ownership
OPALX/IPPL owns Kokkos backend selection. TPS reuses that runtime and any existing
Kokkos Kernels target. If Kernels is absent, it discovers an installed 5.2-or-newer
package or uses release-based FetchContent. It never initializes or finalizes a
second runtime inside OPALX. IPPL owns application initialization/finalization;
all map objects and outstanding kernels must finish before finalization.
The process-isolated numerical tests initialize Kokkos with ScopeGuard instead.

@section storage_execution Fixed and dynamic storage
Fixed frame vectors/matrices use Kokkos::Array. Runtime-sized coefficients,
monomial tables, arrays and Taylor scratch use Kokkos-owned HostSpace allocations.
Array copies are deep/value copies; public unmanaged Views alias live storage.
Nested owning objects have explicit host-side construction/destruction with
WithoutInitializing allocations. This avoids reentrant Kokkos callbacks while
preserving correct lifetime, alignment and exception cleanup. Static cache Views
are released by a finalize hook while the runtime is active.

@section linear_execution Numerical kernels
Matrix/Vector are thin checked host adapters. Products, transforms, scaling,
AXPY, dot and norm use Kokkos Kernels. LUFactorization uses Getrf with partial
pivoting and Getrs, checks INFO and never substitutes a nonpivoted solver.
Matrix(r,c,value) fills all entries; Matrix::identity constructs an identity.
Vector dot is bilinear and does not conjugate; Euclidean norm uses conjugation
for complex inputs. The legacy LU/elimination and BLAS loops are removed.

The algebra's global order/cache still needs external synchronization; OpenMP
support does not make concurrent map construction safe. Device batch evaluation,
GPU performance and full OPALX field-map construction are not established by
this host storage migration. CPU Serial/OpenMP tests exercise numerical
semantics, pivoting, nested lifetimes and fixed-array/default-space View access.
*/

/**
@page prepared_map_api Prepared map and single-particle device evaluation

@section prepared_workflow Host preparation, device evaluation
PreparedMap<T,ExecutionSpace> snapshots a context-bearing Map and uploads its
coefficients and monomial plan to ExecutionSpace::memory_space. T is float or
double; complex/nested coefficient analysis remains a host-algebra capability.
The default execution space is Kokkos::DefaultExecutionSpace. An explicit
execution-space instance selects the upload queue; construction fences that
instance before returning. Metadata strings, chart references and frames remain
host-side. A DeviceMapView holds const-only Views and dimension/workspace sizes.
It contains no host context, dynamic cache or mutable coefficient access.

A source map may be destroyed after preparation. Copies of the device handle
retain its read-only allocations. Keep the handle and all particle/workspace
buffers alive until the kernel execution completes. All owning allocations must
be released before Kokkos finalization. Normal Kokkos runtime ownership applies.

@section monomial_plan Shared monomial recurrence
Preparation omits zero coefficients and retains the active monomials plus the
parents necessary to construct them. With node zero representing one, every
subsequent monomial satisfies
@f[
 m_0=1,\qquad m_i=m_{p_i}x_{v_i},\qquad 0\le p_i<i,
 \qquad F_j(x)=\sum_i C_{ji}m_i.
@f]
The recurrence is topological and computed once for all outputs. For example,
an isolated x^5 term still needs x, x^2, x^3 and x^4 parent nodes. The coefficient
View is output-major LayoutRight; parent and variable indices are read-only
one-dimensional Views. No claim of an optimal Horner plan or maximum throughput
is made. Different source output orders may yield different valid node orders.

For M planned nodes and k outputs, evaluation needs M+k scalar workspace entries.
The additional k entries stage results so errors cannot partially update the
particle. Coefficient storage is k*M scalars plus two M-entry integer arrays.
Constant/zero maps need only the constant node. Unused high-order source
coefficients do not force a full dense monomial evaluation plan.

@section prepared_call Allocation-free primitive
Include only `<tps/DeviceMap.hpp>` in a device-facing interface, or include
`<tps/PreparedMap.hpp>` for host preparation. The umbrella includes both.
`evaluate_one(handle,input,output,workspace)` is KOKKOS_INLINE_FUNCTION and
noexcept. It performs scalar arithmetic without allocations, exceptions, host
cache access, global truncation access, synchronization or particle scheduling.

DeviceSpan is a non-owning pointer/extent/positive-stride descriptor. deviceSpan
adapts rank-one Kokkos Views/subviews and Kokkos::Array. This supports contiguous
and strided particle layouts. The caller must supply actually accessible memory,
representable offsets, correct lifetimes and disjoint workspace for concurrent
particles. Workspace must not alias input/output. Input and output may overlap,
because all input-dependent values are computed before output is committed.

@section prepared_status Status and output guarantees
EvaluationStatus reports success, unpreparedMap, inputSizeMismatch,
outputSizeMismatch, workspaceTooSmall, invalidBuffer, nonFiniteInput or
nonFiniteResult. Shape/capacity checks precede any writes. Nonfinite monomials or
output accumulations return nonFiniteResult. Every reported failure leaves
output unchanged; workspace contents are unspecified after arithmetic failure.
Preparation rejects nonfinite source coefficients on the host.

These checks do not certify the map's physical validity region, aperture status,
truncation error or symplecticity. Coordinates remain local chart coordinates;
reference/frame/unit conversions belong to the OPALX adapter. Kernel tests
compare host and analytic values with existing 2e-13 double tolerance. Float
checks use 3e-6 for these bounded low-order examples, allowing ordinary float
roundoff; neither is a universal accuracy bound. Term ordering/FMA can change
roundoff and bitwise agreement is not promised.

@section prepared_example Single-particle example
The example is a compiled test. The demonstration launch is not an OPALX batch
scheduler; OPALX will choose policy, particle Views, workspace location, validity
handling and collective-kick orchestration after integration.
@include prepared_evaluation.cpp
*/

/**
@page error_contracts Errors and borrowed storage

@section algebra_errors Host algebra
Include `<tps/Error.hpp>`. All algebra error types derive directly from tps::Error,
which derives from std::runtime_error. what() describes the failure and where()
identifies the operation. CLRangeError denotes invalid indices/orders; SizeError
incompatible dimensions; DivideError zero division; DomainError an elementary
function outside its supported domain; FormatError malformed serialized input;
LogicalError invalid algebra preconditions; SingularMatrixError a singular solve.
The intermediate ArithmeticError and historical ClassicException names are removed.
Map metadata and prepared-map validation use standard exceptions. Allocation and
coefficient-operation exceptions may propagate; this is not a strong exception
guarantee for the legacy cache or all compound algebra operations.

@section borrowed_storage Borrowed arrays and device buffers
Array1D/Array2D have value semantics: copying owns independent storage. Their
view()/flatView() methods return unmanaged HostSpace Kokkos Views. These do not
extend the owner's lifetime. Complete all uses before resizing, assigning or
destroying the array. Raw row pointers and iterators have the same requirement.
These host adapters are not device-accessible storage on every backend.

DeviceMapView instead retains ownership of its read-only allocations through
managed Views. DeviceSpan does not own its buffer. Its valid() checks pointer and
positive stride metadata, not allocation extent, memory accessibility, overlap
or offset overflow. The adapter must establish those conditions before launching.
EvaluationStatus reports only the checks in evaluate_one; it is not an error
estimate, symplecticity proof, aperture test or particle-loss decision.
*/
